Lung cancer originates from the tissues of the lung, usually from cells lining the air passages.
It is part of our respiratory system.
During breathing, the diaphragm moves up and down and carries air in and out of the lungs.
The trachea divides into two parts and enters the right lung.
They are covered by a thin covering called ‘pleura’ which protects and helps lungs move back and forth as they expand and contract during breathing.
Air enters the lungs through nose or mouth via windpipe (trachea) which divides into two airways going into the right and left lung each.
Lung cancer is mainly divided into two parts - NSCCL and S.C.L.C. (Depending on the composition of the tumor cells) N.S.C.L.C. cancer (75-80%) Cancer is found to be higher than that of (15–20 percent).
The smoker to non-smoker ratio is high up to 20:1 in various studies.
